1
00:00:00,070 --> 00:00:04,010
From Records, open Reports and select
Saved reports.

2
00:00:04,409 --> 00:00:07,719
This is where you keep a reusable report
instead of rebuilding

3
00:00:07,719 --> 00:00:09,340
the same query each time.

4
00:00:09,760 --> 00:00:13,720
The page explains that a saved report
queries one definition, can

5
00:00:13,720 --> 00:00:18,090
group or aggregate its records, and can
export the results to C S V.

6
00:00:18,569 --> 00:00:22,200
Results still respect record visibility
and restricted fields.

7
00:00:22,649 --> 00:00:27,200
Select New report to begin a separate
fictional community coordination report.

8
00:00:27,530 --> 00:00:30,350
Give the report a recognizable name and
description.

9
00:00:30,870 --> 00:00:33,999
Select the definition that owns the
records you want to review.

10
00:00:34,349 --> 00:00:37,440
Here it is the training community software
exercise.

11
00:00:37,830 --> 00:00:40,809
The Columns panel then offers record
fields and that

12
00:00:40,809 --> 00:00:42,490
definition’s detail fields.

13
00:00:42,799 --> 00:00:46,700
Select record dot number, record dot
state, and Summary.

14
00:00:47,060 --> 00:00:49,839
These three choices become the three
result columns.

15
00:00:50,169 --> 00:00:52,409
Leave Notes unselected for this example.

16
00:00:52,810 --> 00:00:56,739
A clear report name helps supervisors
choose the right recurring review.

17
00:00:56,960 --> 00:01:00,630
Set Sort by to record dot number and check
Descending.

18
00:01:01,039 --> 00:01:05,779
The first version is a detail report, so
leave Group by and Aggregates empty.

19
00:01:06,170 --> 00:01:09,980
This makes the newest training number
appear first in the recorded result.

20
00:01:10,310 --> 00:01:14,539
Sorting determines the order of rows;
grouping is a separate decision.

21
00:01:14,960 --> 00:01:18,859
Later we will edit this same report to
count records by state without

22
00:01:18,859 --> 00:01:20,480
replacing the detail columns.

23
00:01:20,679 --> 00:01:21,980
Scroll to Filters.

24
00:01:22,470 --> 00:01:28,509
This example uses a J S O N array with
FieldKey set to record dot state, Operator

25
00:01:28,509 --> 00:01:31,610
set to Equals, and Value set to Finalized.

26
00:01:32,060 --> 00:01:34,860
Match the quoted text and brackets shown
in the form.

27
00:01:35,289 --> 00:01:39,080
The inline example and schema help explain
the expected structure.

28
00:01:39,549 --> 00:01:41,439
Set Max rows to twenty-five.

29
00:01:42,029 --> 00:01:45,980
Leave Include drafts and Include
restricted fields unchecked.

30
00:01:46,410 --> 00:01:49,019
Leave Version mappings empty for this
exercise.

31
00:01:49,449 --> 00:01:52,890
These choices keep this report focused on
a small, permitted

32
00:01:52,890 --> 00:01:54,559
set of finalized records.

33
00:01:54,720 --> 00:01:56,749
Select Validate before saving.

34
00:01:57,200 --> 00:02:00,939
The page returns with The report is valid
above the prepared

35
00:02:00,939 --> 00:02:02,720
settings and selected columns.

36
00:02:03,140 --> 00:02:06,749
Validation checks the report definition;
it is not a result

37
00:02:06,749 --> 00:02:08,720
count and does not replace Save.

38
00:02:09,129 --> 00:02:11,900
Return to the bottom controls and select
Save.

39
00:02:12,320 --> 00:02:17,820
The Saved reports page now shows Report
saved, the report name, its definition,

40
00:02:18,109 --> 00:02:20,129
three columns, and the Run action.

41
00:02:20,480 --> 00:02:24,740
That saved row is the first readback that
the reusable report exists.

42
00:02:24,880 --> 00:02:26,840
Select Run on the saved row.

43
00:02:27,290 --> 00:02:31,329
The result shows two of two rows, with
Record number, State

44
00:02:31,399 --> 00:02:32,970
and Summary as configured.

45
00:02:33,440 --> 00:02:37,119
Both records are Finalized, and the larger
training number is first.

46
00:02:37,529 --> 00:02:41,619
Compare those values with the purpose of
the report before distributing its output.

47
00:02:42,119 --> 00:02:45,830
Use the C S V button on this result page
to download the data.

48
00:02:46,330 --> 00:02:50,439
In this exercise the downloaded file
contains the same three headers and the

49
00:02:50,439 --> 00:02:52,810
same two fictional records shown here.

50
00:02:52,959 --> 00:02:55,819
Select Edit and review the persisted
settings.

51
00:02:56,120 --> 00:03:00,269
The three column choices and descending
record-number sort are still present.

52
00:03:00,639 --> 00:03:03,270
Set Group by to record dot state.

53
00:03:03,670 --> 00:03:08,959
In Aggregates, enter a J S O N array whose
object has Aggregate set to Count,

54
00:03:09,279 --> 00:03:11,810
exactly as the form’s Count example shows.

55
00:03:12,119 --> 00:03:16,679
This asks a different question: how many
matching records are in each state?

56
00:03:17,029 --> 00:03:19,519
The original detail columns remain
selected.

57
00:03:19,760 --> 00:03:23,180
Validate the changed report and confirm
The report is valid.

58
00:03:23,629 --> 00:03:27,549
Use the same bottom Save control, then run
the saved report again.

59
00:03:28,060 --> 00:03:31,480
The updated index identifies Group by
record dot state

60
00:03:31,690 --> 00:03:33,299
and shows a Last run time.

61
00:03:33,720 --> 00:03:37,870
The new result has a Groups section:
Finalized has a count of two.

62
00:03:38,280 --> 00:03:41,239
The detail section still shows the same
two rows.

63
00:03:41,639 --> 00:03:45,850
Check both sections so that the summary
count can be traced back to its records.

64
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:49,519
Use this pattern for the records your
department actually maintains.

65
00:03:49,940 --> 00:03:53,650
A community paramedicine or mental health
response team can review

66
00:03:53,650 --> 00:03:55,449
completed coordination records.

67
00:03:55,810 --> 00:04:00,050
Search-and-rescue teams can summarize
activity forms, security departments

68
00:04:00,050 --> 00:04:05,020
can review service records, and wildland
contractors or industrial emergency teams

69
00:04:05,020 --> 00:04:07,030
can track their operational paperwork.

70
00:04:07,409 --> 00:04:10,520
Fire and E M S departments can use the
same controls.

71
00:04:10,959 --> 00:04:15,479
Choose the appropriate definition, select
only needed fields, validate

72
00:04:15,479 --> 00:04:19,220
the settings, and verify the detail behind
each grouped total.

